Description: WebXR flat display: the separation is the screen's limit in the window's own pixels, never the window's width (ORDER 750d)
 The person's depth limits are set once, for the screen (Daniel, 2026-11-19: "one separation setting, centralized, tailored TO THE
 SCREEN not to the element size"), in pixels of a view as wide as the output. An inline-stereo session (WebXR 3.4) shows its two
 views through the page's canvas, at the canvas's own width on the output, and GetWindowViews gave the eyes a separation that was
 that limit over 1920 times the canvas's width (published +stereo3d15 in the rig: the farthest point of a tilted object at 9.94
 px on the screen with a canvas 1904 wide, 4.06 px with a canvas 890 wide). The separation is now the share of the window that
 is the output's width over the width the window is shown at, the same conversion as the WebGL camera (sunk / 1920 * output
 width / canvas width), with the output's width from the same source (widget::StereoDepthProcessLimits, the screen when none is
 known). An immersive session fills the output, so its ratio is one and it is unchanged.
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 via Claude Code
